  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{10x+2y}{5x+y+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=5x+y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{\u2032}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2(5x+y)}{(5x+y)+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=5x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=5+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\u27f9\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-5 & =\\frac{2z}{z+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{\u2032}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{7z+5}{z+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{7z+5}{z+1}.$",
